---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Rule from "../components/Rule.astro";
import Sources from "../components/Sources.astro";
import SovereigntyTimeline from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/SovereigntyTimeline.astro";
import Timeline from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Timeline.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import sov from "../data/sovereignty.json";
import pressures from "../data/pressures.json";
import Ribbon from "../components/Ribbon.astro";

/* Blazevic sets both halves down together and it reads better than either
   alone: what this family did, among what was being done to it. World entries
   are stored as [year, text] pairs here as elsewhere in the estate. */
const world = (sov.world || [])
  .map((w) => (Array.isArray(w) ? { year: w[0], text: w[1] } : w));
const rows = [
  ...sov.events.map((e) => ({ year: e.year, when: String(e.year), title: e.text,
                              kind: "this family", big: true })),
  ...world.map((e) => ({ year: e.year, when: String(e.year), title: e.text,
                         kind: "the world around them" })),
];
---
<Base title="The Timeline" eyebrow="Lives against history"
      dek="Sovereignty, pressure and family event on one scale. The point is not when people were born — it is what was happening to them when they were.">

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The family, and the world it was in</h2>
  <p class="dek">Scroll sideways. Above the line, this family. Below it, what was happening around them. The point of putting them on one scale is that 1949 is the year one brother sailed for Australia and the other bought a second car.</p>
  <div style="margin-top:24px"><Ribbon /></div>
  <p class="cap">Drag or scroll the band horizontally. 1340 to 2030.</p>
</section>
<Rule name="arrows" />

<section class="blk">
  <SovereigntyTimeline sov={sov} events={true} />
  <Timeline events={rows} u={u} />
</section>
<Rule name="pomegranate" />

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What they were up against</h2>
  <p class="dek">War, plague, famine and policy, on the same scale. A life bar crossing one of these is the beginning of a question worth asking.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m">Years</th><th>Pressure</th><th>Falls on</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{pressures.map(p=>(
        <tr><td class="m">{p.from}–{p.to}</td><td style="color:var(--ink)">{p.label}</td>
        <td>{p.lane==="all"?"All three regions":p.lane==="istria"?"Istria":"Senj & Vinodol"}</td></tr>
      ))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:26px">Once the GEDCOM is imported, each life becomes a bar drawn over these bands, so that a man born at Gračišće in 1935 is shown becoming Italian, then Yugoslav, before he was ten. That view needs people; the frame is ready for them.</div>
</section>
  <Sources ids={["carlo1879", "kobler", "familysearch"]} />
</Base>
